State the repository limits the section
lists and find the commit that added a large

32
00:02:22,045 --> 00:02:22,311
file.

33
00:02:24,151 --> 00:02:29,348
Issues. In one sentence: an issue is a
numbered record in GitHub's database for a

34
00:02:29,388 --> 00:02:34,649
unit of work or a report, which since 2025
can have a type, a parent, children and

35
00:02:34,689 --> 00:02:35,489
dependencies.

36
00:02:35,829 --> 00:02:40,897
To the REST API, "every pull request is
an issue, but not every issue is a pull

37
00:02:40,937 --> 00:02:46,199
request". That's why #12 can be either. A
pull request, remember, is a proposal to

38
00:02:46,239 --> 00:02:48,138
merge one branch into another.

39
00:02:48,478 --> 00:02:54,463
The structure added in 2025 and 2026.
Sub-issues: up to 100 per parent, eight levels

40
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:58,479
deep, generally available
since the ninth of April 2025.

41
00:02:58,519 --> 00:03:04,217
Issue types: defined per organization, up
to 25, with the defaults task, bug and

42
00:03:04,257 --> 00:03:10,170
feature. Dependencies, "blocked by" and
"blocking": up to 50 links per relationship

43
00:03:10,210 --> 00:03:15,979
type, since the twenty-first of August
2025. Issue fields: typed metadata defined

44
00:03:16,019 --> 00:03:21,287
per organization, with four default
fields, since the second of July 2026.

45
00:03:21,327 --> 00:03:26,809
Types and fields belong to an organization:
one more thing a personal account

46
00:03:26,849 --> 00:03:27,742
doesn't have.

47
00:03:28,082 --> 00:03:33,883
Closing keywords. Nine words link a pull
request or a commit to an issue: close,

48
00:03:33,923 --> 00:03:39,871
closes, closed, fix, fixes, fixed, resolve,
resolves, resolved, followed by #N for

49
00:03:39,911 --> 00:03:45,421
the same repository or OWNER/REPO#N for
another, in any letter case, with an

50
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:46,516
optional colon.

51
00:03:46,856 --> 00:03:50,285
Three rules explain every "why
did it not close" question.

52
00:03:50,325 --> 00:03:55,189
One: in a pull request description the
keywords are interpreted only when the pull

53
00:03:55,229 --> 00:03:59,675
request targets the default branch, the
branch a repository presents first.

54
00:03:59,715 --> 00:04:04,280
Against any other branch they're ignored:
no link is made and nothing closes.

55
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:08,885
Two: in a commit message the issue closes
when the commit reaches the default

56
00:04:08,925 --> 00:04:13,431
branch, and the pull request that carried
the commit isn't listed as linked.

57
00:04:13,471 --> 00:04:17,916
Three: the issue closes at merge time,
not when the pull request is opened.

58
00:04:18,256 --> 00:04:23,113
Quick quiz. A commit message says "Fixes
#812", and the commit sits on a feature

59
00:04:23,153 --> 00:04:28,193
branch. When does the issue close: when the
commit is pushed, when the pull request

60
00:04:28,233 --> 00:04:31,437
opens, or when the commit
reaches the default branch?

61
00:04:31,477 --> 00:04:32,416
Say it out loud.

62
00:04:37,256 --> 00:04:40,885
When the commit reaches the
default branch. That's rule two.

63
00:04:40,925 --> 00:04:45,960
If you said "when the pull request opens",
rule three is the one to reread: opening

64
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:47,795
a pull request closes nothing.

65
00:04:48,135 --> 00:04:52,708
And the layer: the keyword is text in a
commit object or in a database field.

66
00:04:52,748 --> 00:04:56,242
Git does nothing with it. It
also travels with the message.

67
00:04:56,282 --> 00:05:01,154
A cherry-picked or rebased copy of the
commit carries the same words, and they act

68
00:05:01,194 --> 00:05:03,551
when that copy reaches a default branch.

69
00:05:03,891 --> 00:05:10,061
For daily work, gh issue create,
list, view, edit, close and develop.

70
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:17,621
A version caveat: the installed GitHub CLI
2.88.1 has no flags for types, parents or

71
00:05:17,661 --> 00:05:23,741
dependencies. gh issue create --type
and --parent arrived in 2.94.0.

72
00:05:24,081 --> 00:05:29,271
Labels and milestones. A label classifies
issues, pull requests and discussions

73
00:05:29,311 --> 00:05:34,766
within one repository. Creating a label
needs Write, and applying one needs Triage.

74
00:05:34,806 --> 00:05:40,194
Labels are more than decoration when
something reads them: an issue form can apply

75
00:05:40,234 --> 00:05:45,425
labels, generated release notes group
pull requests by label, and workflows can

76
00:05:45,465 --> 00:05:50,257
react to them. A milestone groups
issues and pull requests toward a date.

77
00:05:50,297 --> 00:05:55,620
It is not a Git tag and not a release: a
milestone called v0.3.0 promises nothing

78
00:05:55,660 --> 00:05:57,275
about which commit ships.

79
00:05:57,615 --> 00:06:02,912
Projects is the planning layer: "an adaptable
table, board, and roadmap" over issues

80
00:06:02,952 --> 00:06:07,676
and pull requests, owned by a user or
an organization, not by a repository.

81
00:06:07,716 --> 00:06:12,886
An outdated-advice note: "Projects (classic)",
a board attached to one repository,

82
00:06:12,926 --> 00:06:16,698
was sunset on GitHub.com on the
twenty-third of August 2024.

83
00:06:17,038 --> 00:06:21,727
Discussions is a forum beside the issue
tracker. The working rule from the textbook:

84
00:06:21,767 --> 00:06:26,345
an issue can be done and closed. A question
or an open-ended idea is a discussion.

85
00:06:26,685 --> 00:06:29,700
A wiki is documentation in
a second Git repository.

86
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:34,494
Because it's a separate repository, a wiki
isn't reviewed through pull requests,

87
00:06:34,534 --> 00:06:39,347
isn't covered by the rulesets of the main
repository, and isn't included when you

88
00:06:39,387 --> 00:06:44,320
clone or mirror the code. Documentation
that must be reviewed with the code belongs

89
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:44,859
in docs/.

90
00:06:45,199 --> 00:06:51,591
Packages. GitHub Packages hosts registries
for npm, RubyGems, Maven, Gradle, Docker

91
00:06:51,631 --> 00:06:58,023
and NuGet. The Container registry at ghcr.io
is the home for Docker and OCI images.

92
00:06:58,363 --> 00:07:03,459
Four facts. A package is not Git data and
not part of a repository: an image is "not

93
00:07:03,499 --> 00:07:08,289
linked to a repository by default".
Authentication is an exception to the token

94
00:07:08,329 --> 00:07:13,181
advice of the next videos: "GitHub Packages
only supports authentication using a

95
00:07:13,221 --> 00:07:18,255
personal access token (classic)", while
inside a workflow the job's GITHUB_TOKEN is

96
00:07:18,295 --> 00:07:18,561
used.

97
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:24,485
Public packages are free. And provenance
is separate from storage: an artifact

98
00:07:24,525 --> 00:07:30,469
attestation is a signed claim about which
workflow run built an artifact from which

99
00:07:30,509 --> 00:07:36,308
commit. The old registry docker.pkg.github.com
was closed on the twenty-fourth of

100
00:07:36,348 --> 00:07:38,255
February 2025. Use ghcr.io.

101
00:07:38,595 --> 00:07:43,651
Health files. In one sentence: community
health files are tracked files with

102
00:07:43,691 --> 00:07:49,215
well-known names that GitHub finds and uses
to guide the people who interact with a

103
00:07:49,255 --> 00:07:54,713
repository. GitHub looks for each file in
.github/, then the repository root, then

104
00:07:54,753 --> 00:07:55,115
docs/.

105
00:07:55,455 --> 00:08:00,080
README.md is rendered on the front page.
LICENSE: GitHub detects and shows the

106
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:05,104
license. Without one, default copyright
applies and nobody is permitted to reuse the

107
00:08:05,144 --> 00:08:05,403
code.

108
00:08:05,743 --> 00:08:10,624
CONTRIBUTING.md is linked when someone
opens an issue or pull request.

109
00:08:10,664 --> 00:08:16,036
SECURITY.md is shown as the security
policy. CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md counts in the

110
00:08:16,076 --> 00:08:17,301
community profile.

111
00:08:17,641 --> 00:08:22,569
The files under .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/
are offered in the "new issue" chooser.

112
00:08:22,609 --> 00:08:27,160
pull_request_template.md pre-fills the
description of a new pull request.

113
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:32,254
Both templates are read from the default
branch. Unlike settings, these files are

114
00:08:32,294 --> 00:08:34,329
versioned and reviewed like code.

115
00:08:34,669 --> 00:08:39,410
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
GitHub's syntax page still carries the

116
00:08:39,450 --> 00:08:44,192
note that issue forms are in public preview,
and the course's research could not

117
00:08:44,232 --> 00:08:47,061
determine whether that
note is current or stale.

118
00:08:47,401 --> 00:08:52,453
Limits. GitHub's limits come in three
kinds: enforced, recommended, and display

119
00:08:52,493 --> 00:08:56,901
limits. Mixing them up produces both
needless worry and real outages.

120
00:08:57,241 --> 00:09:03,229
Enforced: one file in a push gives a warning
above 50 mebibytes and is blocked above

121
00:09:03,269 --> 00:09:07,463
100 mebibytes. One file added
in the browser: 25 mebibytes.

122
00:09:07,503 --> 00:09:11,410
One push: 2 gigabytes. "Rebase
and merge": 100 commits.

123
00:09:11,450 --> 00:09:15,572
Release assets: 1,000 per
release, each under 2 gibibytes.

124
00:09:15,912 --> 00:09:21,247
Recommended: repository size "ideally less
than 1 GB, and less than 5 GB is strongly

125
00:09:21,287 --> 00:09:26,238
recommended" on one page, and 10 gigabytes
on disk as a recommended maximum on

126
00:09:26,278 --> 00:09:31,550
another. The two sit on two pages and differ.
Treat 1 gigabyte as the target and 10

127
00:09:31,590 --> 00:09:34,941
gigabytes as the point where
GitHub expects problems.

128
00:09:35,281 --> 00:09:41,128
Also recommended: 3,000 entries in one
directory, a directory depth of 50, 5,000

129
00:09:41,168 --> 00:09:47,014
branches. Display: a pull request diff of
20,000 lines or 1 megabyte that can be

130
00:09:47,054 --> 00:09:52,754
loaded, and 300 files. And 250 commits
listed in a pull request or comparison.

131
00:09:53,094 --> 00:09:57,865
The file limit applies to every object
a push sends, not to the files in your

132
00:09:57,905 --> 00:10:03,050
working tree. A file you deleted in a later
commit is still in history, and a first

133
00:10:03,090 --> 00:10:04,674
push sends all of history.

134
00:10:05,014 --> 00:10:09,581
Governance in brief, each item a
GitHub object that no clone carries.

135
00:10:09,621 --> 00:10:14,187
Base permission, teams and roles.
Required two-factor authentication.

136
00:10:14,227 --> 00:10:19,394
A personal access token policy. Repository
rulesets and organization rulesets.

137
00:10:19,434 --> 00:10:23,266
Custom properties. The audit
log, 180 days and searchable.

138
00:10:23,306 --> 00:10:28,473
And on Enterprise Cloud, SAML single
sign-on, IP allow lists, custom roles and

139
00:10:28,513 --> 00:10:33,212
enterprise policies. One caveat marked
unverified in the book: GitHub's

140
00:10:33,252 --> 00:10:38,820
documentation contradicts itself on whether
organization rulesets need Enterprise or

141
00:10:38,860 --> 00:10:43,025
Team, and the course prefers the
later sources, which say Team.

142
00:10:44,865 --> 00:10:49,617
Keep the manuscript in the publisher's
office from video 115, and remember where

143
00:10:49,657 --> 00:10:54,409
that analogy broke: the office reads
instructions written inside the manuscript.

144
00:10:54,749 --> 00:10:59,262
This whole video is that break, item by
item. The issue form, the pull request

145
00:10:59,302 --> 00:11:03,581
template, the security policy, the
closing keyword: each is written in the

146
00:11:03,621 --> 00:11:06,792
manuscript, in Git, and acted
on by the office, GitHub.

147
00:11:06,832 --> 00:11:11,461
So each has two halves you can check
separately. Is the text there, in the right

148
00:11:11,501 --> 00:11:16,014
place, on the right branch? That's a Git
question. Did the platform act on it?

149
00:11:16,054 --> 00:11:20,100
That's a GitHub question, and its
conditions are in the documentation.

150
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:24,060
When a platform feature "does not
work", check the Git half first.

151
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,501
It's the half you can
prove from a terminal.

152
00:11:28,341 --> 00:11:31,333
This layout is a convention,
not a GitHub requirement.

153
00:11:31,373 --> 00:11:34,309
GitHub reads the health files
and nothing else in it.

154
00:11:34,649 --> 00:11:38,125
Go down the tree and ask of
each line: who reads it?

155
00:11:38,165 --> 00:11:43,601
.github/workflows/ is read by GitHub
Actions. Write can change what runs with the

156
00:11:43,641 --> 00:11:48,469
repository's secrets, which is why
this directory deserves a code owner.

157
00:11:48,509 --> 00:11:52,119
CODEOWNERS is only a request
until a rule requires it.

158
00:11:52,459 --> 00:11:57,084
.gitignore is read by Git, and it
doesn't untrack what is already tracked.

159
00:11:57,124 --> 00:12:02,064
LICENSE is read by GitHub and by lawyers:
without it nobody outside may use the

160
00:12:02,104 --> 00:12:07,044
code, whatever the visibility. SECURITY.md
exists so that the first report of a

161
00:12:07,084 --> 00:12:09,250
vulnerability isn't a public issue.

162
00:12:13,590 --> 00:12:19,171
The diagram has a .github/workflows/
directory. Will git ls-files list it?

163
00:12:19,211 --> 00:12:21,222
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

164
00:12:26,062 --> 00:12:31,481
It isn't there. Git tracks files, not
directories, so the directory appears with the

165
00:12:31,521 --> 00:12:33,496
first workflow file, in Part 6.

166
00:12:36,836 --> 00:12:41,775
An issue form is a YAML file that turns
the free-text issue into a form with

167
00:12:41,815 --> 00:12:46,754
required fields. Point at labels: ["bug"]:
the form applies a default label.

168
00:12:46,794 --> 00:12:52,127
Point at the input element that asks for
the output of git rev-parse --short HEAD:

169
00:12:52,167 --> 00:12:57,499
the form asks the reporter for a commit
ID. The file was parsed with a YAML parser

170
00:12:57,539 --> 00:13:00,447
for the course. Only
GitHub validates a form.

171
00:13:00,787 --> 00:13:05,956
blank_issues_enabled: false removes the
blank issue from the chooser for people with

172
00:13:05,996 --> 00:13:09,428
the Read or Triage role. People
with Write still see it.

173
00:13:09,468 --> 00:13:14,264
The pull request template is plain
Markdown. Content inside an HTML comment is

174
00:13:14,304 --> 00:13:19,225
hidden in the rendered description, so
the comment guides the author and doesn't

175
00:13:19,265 --> 00:13:23,194
clutter the result. Issue forms
can't be used for pull requests.

176
00:13:23,534 --> 00:13:25,391
Two checks that belong to the layout.

177
00:13:25,731 --> 00:13:30,044
A new file named .env. What
does git status --short print?

178
00:13:30,084 --> 00:13:31,621
Make your prediction.

179
00:13:36,461 --> 00:13:41,372
Nothing: the file is ignored. git check-ignore
-v names the rule: the file, the line

180
00:13:41,412 --> 00:13:46,323
number and the pattern. A local secret file
must be ignored before the first commit.

181
00:13:46,663 --> 00:13:50,133
Try it now, thirty seconds,
in a repository of your own.

182
00:13:50,173 --> 00:13:54,521
Pick a file you believe is ignored,
and run git check-ignore -v on it.

183
00:13:54,561 --> 00:13:55,148
I'll wait.

184
00:13:59,988 --> 00:14:04,755
You should see the same three parts as on
screen: a file of rules, a line number and

185
00:14:04,795 --> 00:14:05,328
a pattern.

186
00:14:05,668 --> 00:14:10,193
The tests run from a fresh clone with one
documented command and leave the working

187
00:14:10,233 --> 00:14:10,806
tree clean.

188
00:14:13,646 --> 00:14:17,662
A model file was added in one commit
and moved out of Git in the next.

189
00:14:17,702 --> 00:14:20,270
No tracked file has
"classifier" in its name.

190
00:14:20,610 --> 00:14:25,645
The file is gone from the working tree.
Is it gone from what a first push would

191
00:14:25,685 --> 00:14:27,765
send? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

192
00:14:32,605 --> 00:14:36,900
It's the largest blob of the repository:
3 mebibytes, still reachable.

193
00:14:36,940 --> 00:14:41,978
If you said gone, that's the natural answer,
and it's the one behind many rejected

194
00:14:42,018 --> 00:14:44,517
pushes. This is the pipeline from Part 4.

195
00:14:44,857 --> 00:14:49,907
--diff-filter=A finds the commit
that added the path: c7182c9.

196
00:14:50,247 --> 00:14:55,678
Here's the picture. The branch main points
at 39c4105, the commit that removed the

197
00:14:55,718 --> 00:15:01,016
file. Behind it sits c7182c9, which added
it, and a first push sends all of that

198
00:15:01,056 --> 00:15:06,153
history. Had the file been 300 mebibytes,
the push would be rejected for that

199
00:15:06,193 --> 00:15:10,423
commit. The remedies are rewriting
unpushed history, or Git LFS.

200
00:15:10,463 --> 00:15:14,226
For a model file the better
answer is usually a registry.

201
00:15:14,566 --> 00:15:19,342
The lab creates a free organization in the
browser, copies the practice repository

202
00:15:19,382 --> 00:15:23,689
template, and makes the first commit
locally. Then one command creates the

203
00:15:23,729 --> 00:15:28,035
repository in the organization and
pushes. Replace YOUR-ORG with your own.

204
00:15:28,375 --> 00:15:34,456
Say what each step created. gh repo create
created a GitHub object, the repository,

205
00:15:34,496 --> 00:15:38,586
added a remote to your
.git/config, and pushed Git data.

206
00:15:38,626 --> 00:15:42,422
gh repo edit changed three
settings: GitHub objects.

207
00:15:42,762 --> 00:15:47,705
Now show the files taking effect after
the push. With gh issue create --web, the

208
00:15:47,745 --> 00:15:52,813
browser opens the chooser: according to
the documentation, it offers the two forms

209
00:15:52,853 --> 00:15:56,738
by their name and description,
and no blank issue for a reader.

210
00:15:56,778 --> 00:16:01,099
Open the bug form: the required fields
are the ones the YAML declared.

211
00:16:01,439 --> 00:16:06,185
Close the tab without creating an issue.
Then start a pull request from any branch

212
00:16:06,225 --> 00:16:10,270
in the browser: the description field
is pre-filled with the template.

213
00:16:10,310 --> 00:16:14,998
Finally open the community profile, under
Insights, Community Standards, and read

214
00:16:15,038 --> 00:16:16,924
the checklist against your files.

215
00:16:18,764 --> 00:16:20,412
Five mistakes to watch for.

216
00:16:20,752 --> 00:16:25,795
Expecting "Fixes #N" to close an issue
from a pull request into a release branch.

217
00:16:25,835 --> 00:16:30,816
Root cause: keywords in a pull request
description are interpreted only when the

218
00:16:30,856 --> 00:16:33,326
pull request targets the default branch.

219
00:16:33,666 --> 00:16:37,069
Editing a template on a feature
branch and seeing no change.

220
00:16:37,109 --> 00:16:41,833
Root cause: issue templates and the pull
request template are read from the default

221
00:16:41,873 --> 00:16:42,234
branch.

222
00:16:42,574 --> 00:16:46,737
Deleting a large file in a new commit
to make a rejected push succeed.

223
00:16:46,777 --> 00:16:51,779
Root cause: the limit applies to every object
a push sends, and the file is still in

224
00:16:51,819 --> 00:16:52,260
history.

225
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:57,163
Keeping reviewed documentation in the
wiki. Root cause: the wiki is a second

226
00:16:57,203 --> 00:17:01,766
repository, outside pull requests,
rulesets, clones and mirrors of the code.

227
00:17:02,106 --> 00:17:05,558
Reading a milestone named
after a version as a release.

228
00:17:05,598 --> 00:17:10,574
Root cause: a milestone is a GitHub
grouping toward a date; it names no commit.

229
00:17:12,414 --> 00:17:16,799
Now, out of the lab. The team from
the hook ships from release branches.

230
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:21,224
After the incident with issue 812,
they check the three causes in order.

231
00:17:21,264 --> 00:17:25,588
Did the pull request target the default
branch? It didn't: cause found.

232
00:17:25,628 --> 00:17:28,415
Had it targeted main,
two checks would remain.

233
00:17:28,755 --> 00:17:33,253
Was the keyword in the pull request
description, or only in a commit message?

234
00:17:33,293 --> 00:17:38,146
In that case the pull request isn't listed
as linked, and the issue closes when the

235
00:17:38,186 --> 00:17:42,036
commit reaches the default branch.
And had the merge happened yet?

236
00:17:42,076 --> 00:17:43,863
The issue closes at merge time.

237
00:17:44,203 --> 00:17:48,585
Their fix is a process decision
written down where contributors look.

238
00:17:48,625 --> 00:17:53,968
Teams that ship from release branches close
issues by hand or by automation, and say

239
00:17:54,008 --> 00:17:55,378
so in CONTRIBUTING.md.

240
00:17:57,218 --> 00:18:02,452
Your turn. Do Lab 19.1, "The practice
organization and a repository with health

241
00:18:02,492 --> 00:18:07,393
files", in lab-manual/m19-github-platform.md.
Run it in your normal shell.

242
00:18:07,433 --> 00:18:12,801
Before each numbered step, write down what
it will create in Git and what it will

243
00:18:12,841 --> 00:18:18,142
create on GitHub. Before you open the
issue chooser, predict what it will offer.

244
00:18:18,482 --> 00:18:25,018
The challenge is Exercise 19.7, "We
made it private, so we are fine", in

245
00:18:25,058 --> 00:18:27,576
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

246
00:18:29,416 --> 00:18:32,686
Question 228 of the CTO question bank:

247
00:18:35,026 --> 00:18:39,638
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives
three distinct causes that follow from

248
00:18:39,678 --> 00:18:44,652
documented rules, not three guesses, and
for each one the observation that confirms

249
00:18:44,692 --> 00:18:49,546
or excludes it. It says which layer holds
the keyword and which layer acts on it.

250
00:18:49,586 --> 00:18:54,440
It ends with what a team that merges into
non-default branches should do instead.

251
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:57,207
Let's land this.

252
00:18:57,547 --> 00:18:59,207
You should now be able to say:

253
00:18:59,547 --> 00:19:04,368
An issue is a GitHub record; a closing
keyword is text that GitHub acts on at merge

254
00:19:04,408 --> 00:19:06,359
time, for the default branch only.

255
00:19:06,699 --> 00:19:11,154
Projects plan, Discussions host open-ended
questions, a wiki is a separate

256
00:19:11,194 --> 00:19:15,344
repository, and a package belongs
to an account, not to a repository.

257
00:19:15,684 --> 00:19:20,307
Health files and templates are tracked files
in well-known locations, read from the

258
00:19:20,347 --> 00:19:21,150
default branch.

259
00:19:21,490 --> 00:19:25,089
Each file of the standard layout has
a reader and a mistake it prevents.

260
00:19:25,429 --> 00:19:30,870
Limits are enforced, recommended or display
limits; the 100 MiB file limit applies

261
00:19:30,910 --> 00:19:33,276
to history, not to the working tree.

262
00:19:35,116 --> 00:19:42,719
Read sections 15.8 to 15.11, 15.13
to 15.15 and 15.19 of Chapter 15.

263
00:19:42,759 --> 00:19:49,238
Do Exercise 19.6, "Health files
and the issue chooser", in

264
00:19:49,278 --> 00:19:52,386
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

265
00:19:52,726 --> 00:19:57,537
That was a long tour, and at every stop you
can now say which layer you're standing

266
00:19:57,577 --> 00:20:02,446
on. Practise on one page of a repository
you use. Next time, authentication: who you

267
00:20:02,486 --> 00:20:05,719
are, what you may do, and how
Git asks for a credential.

268
00:20:05,759 --> 00:20:08,759
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

269
00:20:08,799 --> 00:20:10,161
See you in the next one.
